        <p class="a-label">Short version</p>
        <p><strong>Thornton is one of the best local SEO markets in the Denver metro, and it is under-contested.</strong> The city grew from 119,391 people in 2010 to roughly 146,689 by 2024 — a 22.9% increase. Rapid in-migration means an unusually high share of households have no established provider for anything.</p>
        <p>Those households search instead of asking a neighbour. Meanwhile competitive density here sits well below Denver proper and Aurora. High intent plus moderate competition is the most efficient combination in local search.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="different">Why Thornton is not just "Denver, further north"</h2>

      <p>Plenty of agencies will sell you a Denver campaign and tell you it covers Thornton. It does not, and the reason is structural rather than technical.</p>

      <p>Google's local algorithm weighs proximity and local entity signals heavily. A business optimised around downtown Denver simply does not carry the same weight for a search performed at 120th and Washington. But there is a more interesting difference than the algorithm, and it is about who lives here.</p>

      <div class="stat-row">
        <div class="stat"><b>34.7</b><span>Median age — younger than the national median</span></div>
        <div class="stat hl"><b>$103,088</b><span>Median household income</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>$482,200</b><span>Median home value</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>31 min</b><span>Average commute each way</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>13.8%</b><span>Work from home</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>7.0%</b><span>Poverty rate</span></div>
      </div>

      <p>Read those together and a specific customer emerges. Thornton skews <strong>young, employed outside the city, and comfortably above the national median income</strong>. Median household income here is $103,088 against a US median of $78,538.</p>

      <p>That is a household forming its first real set of provider relationships. First house, first mortgage, first roof that needs replacing, first kid who needs a dentist. They have money and no habits.</p>

      <p>For a local business, that is the single most valuable customer state there is. And it is the reason Thornton punishes businesses that are invisible online far more than an older, more settled suburb would.</p>

      <h2 id="growth">The growth argument, in numbers</h2>

      <p>This is the part most local marketing advice misses entirely. Population growth does not just add customers. It changes <em>how</em> people find businesses.</p>

