      </div>

      <p>Here is what that curve means commercially. In a settled community, most households already have a plumber they call. Word of mouth dominates and search is a fallback.</p>

      <p>In a city that grew 22.9% in fourteen years, a large share of households are <strong>still choosing</strong>. They have no neighbour to ask, because their neighbour moved in eight months ago too. So they open a phone and search — and whoever appears wins a relationship that can last a decade.</p>

      <div class="callout c-tip">
        <p class="c-label">The practical implication</p>
        <p>In Thornton, a first-page local ranking is not just a lead source. It is a customer acquisition channel with unusually high lifetime value, because you are catching people at the moment they pick a provider for the first time rather than trying to displace someone they already trust.</p>
      </div>

      <p>The city is not finished growing either. Thornton's own economic development materials describe more than <strong>1,400 acres of developable commercial land along the I-25 corridor</strong>, with eight I-25 interchanges and three on E-470. Commercial growth follows rooftops, and there are plenty more coming.</p>

